body.raw:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": 1,
      "answer_text": "One thing that surprised me most about REST design was the importance of idempotency. I learned that methods like PUT and DELETE are designed so that repeating the same request does not create additional side effects. I also found the stateless nature of REST APIs interesting because each request must contain all the information required for processing."
    },
    {
      "question_id": 2,
      "answer_text": "Statelessness is important in REST APIs because the server does not store client session data between requests. Each request from the client contains the required information, which improves scalability and makes distributed systems easier to manage and maintain."
    },
    {
      "question_id": 3,
      "answer_text": "The difference between PUT and POST in REST APIs surprised me because PUT is idempotent while POST is generally not. PUT is usually used for updating or replacing resources, while POST is commonly used to create new resources on the server."
    },
    {
      "question_id": 4,
      "answer_text": "HTTP status codes are important in REST API design because they help clients understand the result of a request. For example, 200 indicates success, 404 means the resource was not found, and 500 indicates a server error response."
    },
    {
      "question_id": 5,
      "answer_text": "Designing REST endpoints can become challenging because developers must balance consistency, scalability, and readability. Endpoint naming conventions, API versioning, and maintaining predictable resource structures are important considerations in REST API design."
    },
    {
      "question_id": 6,
      "answer_text": "REST improves scalability in distributed systems through stateless communication and standardized resource handling. Because each request is independent, servers can process requests more efficiently and caching mechanisms can reduce unnecessary workload."
    }
  ]
}

